# Untitled

## Highly \(\pi\)-extended copolymer as additive-free hole-transport material for perovskite solar cells
Jie Liu\(^{1,2}\), Qianqing Ge\(^{1,2}\), Weifeng Zhang\(^{1,2}\), Jingyuan Ma\(^{1,2}\), Jie Ding\(^{1,2}\), Gui Yu\(^{1,2}\) (✉), and Jinsong Hu\(^{1,2}\) (✉)
\(^{1}\)Beijing National Laboratory for Molecular Sciences, CAS Key Laboratory of Molecular Nanostructure and Nanotechnology, CAS Research/Education Center for Excellence in Molecular Sciences, Institute of Chemistry, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing 100190, China  \(^{2}\)University of Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing 100049, China
\(\copyright\) Tsinghua University Press and Springer-Verlag GmbH Germany 2017
## KEYWORDS
perovskite solar cell, hole-transport layer, dopant-free, PDVT-10, stability
## ABSTRACT
Organolead halide perovskite solar cells have achieved a certified powerconversion efficiency (PCE) of \(22.1\%\) and are thus among the most promising candidates for next-generation photovoltaic devices. To date, most high-efficiency perovskite solar cells have employed arylamine-based hole-transport materials (HTMs), which are expensive and have a low mobility. The complicated doping procedures and the potentially stability-adverse dopants used in these HTMs are among the major bottlenecks for the commercialization of perovskite solar cells (PSCs). Herein, we present a polythiophene-based copolymer (PDVT-10) with a hole mobility up to \(8.2\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{V}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) and a highest occupied molecular orbital level of \(-5.28\;\mathrm{eV}\) as a hole-transport layer (HTL) for a PSC. A device based on this new HTM exhibited a high PCE of \(13.4\%\) under \(100\,\mathrm{mW}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) illumination, which is one of the highest PCEs reported for the dopant-free polymer-based HTLs. Moreover, PDVT-10 exhibited good solution processability, decent air stability, and thermal stability, making it a promising candidate as an HTM for PSCs.
## 1 Introduction
Perovskite solar cells (PSCs) are becoming increasingly popular in the photovoltaic (PV) community and are among the most promising next-generation PV devices [1–6]. Light-absorbing materials of the form \(\mathrm{APbX}_{3}\) (where A is methylammonium (MA) or formamidinium, and X is a halide or their combination) have exhibited extraordinary properties, such as low exciton-binding
energy, few deep-level defects, high carrier mobility, large balanced electron–hole transport lengths, and excellent light-absorption coefficients [7–11]. Since the first report of an organic–inorganic perovskite-based device with a power-conversion efficiency (PCE) of \(3.8\%\) in 2009 [3], there has been a rapid increase in perovskite-based devices, and a PCE of \(22.1\%\) was certified by the National Renewable Energy Laboratory in 2016 [2–5, 7, 12–17].
Despite the significant progress of PSCs with different device structures and absorber fabrication techniques, the hole-transport layers (HTLs) used for high-performance PSCs are limited to two arylamine-based derivatives: \(2^{\prime},2^{\prime}7,7^{\prime}\)-tetrakis(N,N-di-p-methoxyphenylamine)-\(9,9^{\prime}\)-spirobifluorene (spiro-OMeTAD) and poly(triarylamine) (PTAA) [6, 18]. Unfortunately, both pristine spiro-OMeTAD and PTAA films suffer from a low hole mobility \((\sim10^{-4}\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{V}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1})\) and low conductivity \((\sim10^{-5}\ \mathrm{S{\cdot}c m}^{-2})\) [19–24]. Thus, chemical dopants/additives are commonly used to adjust the electrical properties of HTLs [25–28]. Lithium bistrifluoromethylsulfonimidate (Li-TFSI) and 4-tertbutyl pyridine (tBP) were used cooperatively to obtain a homogenous HTL with a high carrier concentration and conductivity [29]. Although these dopants increased the overall \(\mathrm{PCE}\), doped arylamine-based HTLs have some apparent disadvantages: (1) Li-TFSI is hygroscopic and can degrade the perovskite active layer when exposed to the ambient atmosphere [24, 25, 29, 30]; (2) tBP also slowly erodes the perovskite absorber layers [31]; (3) the doping procedures are complicated and the costs are relatively high [20, 26]; and (4) both spiro-OMeTAD and PTAA are very expensive. Therefore, from the viewpoint of long-term stability and future commercialization, these two materials and other arylamine derivatives that require the similar doping process are not suitable for industrial-scale fabrication of PSCs [27, 32–34]. In this regard, it is highly desirable to develop alternative additive-free HTLs with excellent charge-carrier transport characteristics (hole mobility \(>\sim10^{-3}\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{V}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}\)), suitable highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) levels \((-5.1\) to \(-5.3\,\mathrm{eV}\)), and good air-and operation-stability [35–39]. Recently, dopant-free small molecules have been demonstrated as effective hole-transport materials (HTMs) for PSCs. Chuyi et al. used Trux-OMeTAD as an HTM in PSCs and reported a PCE of \(18.6\%\) [36]. Yongsheng et al. fabricated planar PSCs with a PCE of \(14.9\%\) using the D-A conjugated small molecule DOR3T-TBDT [20]. While small-molecule HTLs yield a decent PCE, considering the multi-step synthesis, ultraviolet (UV) stability, structural stability under operational conditions, low purity, and low yield of the small molecules, they are unlikely to be efficient HTMs for the commercialization of PSCs. In addition to small molecules,
soluble polymer semiconductors can satisfy the requirements for large-scale applications. Qiao et al. exploited a diketopyrrolopyrrole-based polymer (PDPP3T) as an HTM in mesoporous devices, achieving a PCE of \(12.3\%\) with slow degradation [40]. Because of their better film-forming ability and structure stability than small molecules, such polymers are promising as HTMs for high-efficiency PSCs.
In this study, we introduced the D-A copolymer poly[2,5-bis(alkyl)pyrrolo[3,4-c]pyrrole-1,4(2H,5H)-dione-alt-5,5'-i(thiophen-2-yl)-2,2'-(E)-2-(2-(thiophen-2-yl)vinyl)thiophene] (hereinafter PDVT-10) [41] as a new additive-free HTM into PSCs with a conventional mesoporous device structure (fluorine-doped tin oxide \((\mathrm{FTO})/\mathrm{c-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{meso-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}/\mathrm{HTL}/\mathrm{Au}\)). PDVT-10 has been used as active layer in field-effect transistors and has yielded remarkable performance as an additive for organic solar cells. Owing to its high hole mobility \((\sim8.2\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{V}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1})\) and HOMO level \((-5.28\,\mathrm{eV})\) that is compatible with the \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) layer, additive-free PDVT-10-based devices exhibited a high efficiency of \(13.4\%\) with an open-circuit voltage (Voc) of \(0.99\,\mathrm{V}\), which is close to that of PTAA-based devices and better than that of HTL-free devices. Moreover, PDVT-10 functioned as both additive-free HTL and protective layer, giving the PSCs a better device performance over long-term exposure in ambient conditions. Because of their solution processability and thermal stability being significantly better than the conventional small molecules, as required by the harsh panel tests, D-A copolymers with a highly \(\pi\)-extended conjugation backbone are a promising class of additive-free HTMs for highly efficient PSCs.
## 2 Experimental
All the starting materials were used as received, without further purification. Methylammonium iodide \(\left(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{I}_{3}\right)\) was synthesized using a previously reported method [42]. An FTO-coated glass substrate (FTO, Pilkington, TEC8) was patterned via laser cutting and cleaned with detergent, diluted water, acetone, and ethanol in an ultrasonic bath for \(30\,\mathrm{min}\) each. The FTO substrates were subjected to UV–Ozone (UVO) treatment for \(20\,\mathrm{min}\) before use. A \(30{-}40\,\mathrm{nm}\)-thick compact \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layer \(\left(\mathrm{c-TiO}_{2}\right)\) was deposited on the
FTO substrate via spray pyrolysis using a titanium diisopropoxide bis-(acetylacetonate) solution (75% in 2-propanol, Sigma-Aldrich) diluted in 1-butanol (1:39, volume ratio) at \(450\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), followed by in situ annealing for \(30\,\mathrm{min}\). A 150–200-nm-thick mesoporous \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layer (meso-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)) was deposited on the \(\mathrm{c-TiO}_{2}\) by spin-coating a \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) solution composed of \(2\,\mathrm{g}\) of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) paste (Dyesol 30 NRD, Dyesol) diluted in \(10\,\mathrm{mL}\) of the anhydrous ethanol solution at 6,000 revolutions per minute (rpm) for \(30\,\mathrm{s}\). After drying at \(125\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(30\,\mathrm{min}\), the as-deposited \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) films were gradually heated to \(525\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in air and maintained at this temperature for \(30\,\mathrm{min}\) to remove the organic components. Before use, the FTO/c-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)/meso-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) substrates were treated with UVO for \(20\,\mathrm{min}\). \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) (prepared by dissolving \(708\,\mathrm{mg}\) of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) into a solvent containing \(1\,\mathrm{mL}\) of dimethylformamide and \(0.167\,\mathrm{mL}\) of dimethyl sulfoxide with vigorous stirring for \(2\,\mathrm{h}\) at room temperature) was spin-coated on the FTO/c-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)/meso-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) substrate at \(3,000\,\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(30\,\mathrm{s}\). Then, a \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{I}\) solution \((50\,\mathrm{mg\cdot mL^{-1}}\) in isopropyl alcohol) was dropped on the wet \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) layer, followed by \(20\,\mathrm{s}\) of waiting before further spin coating at \(3,000\,\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(30\,\mathrm{s}\). Then, the as-prepared films were annealed on a hotplate at \(150\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(20\,\mathrm{min}\). After cooling to room temperature, \(60\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of PDVT-10 \((3\,\mathrm{mg\cdot mL^{-1}}\) in dichlorobenzene) was spin-coated on the perovskite layer at \(3,000\,\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(30\,\mathrm{s}\), followed by further annealing on the hotplate at \(150\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(5\,\mathrm{min}\). For the PTAA-based devices, \(60\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of a PTAA solution was spin-coated on the perovskite layer as the HTL. The PTAA solution was prepared according to previous reports, by dissolving \(15\,\mathrm{mg}\) of PTAA (average \(\mathrm{Mn}\,7,000{-}10,000\) (GPC), Sigma-Aldrich) in \(1.5\,\mathrm{mL}\) of chlorobenzene. After \(20\,\mathrm{min}\) of stirring at room temperature, \(7.5\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of 4-tert-butylpyridine \((96\%,\) Sigma-Aldrich) and \(15\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of a stock solution containing \(170\,\mathrm{mg\cdot mL^{-1}}\) lithium bistrifluoromethylsulfonimidate \((99.95\%,\) Sigma-Aldrich) in acetonitrile \((99.8\%,\) Sigma-Aldrich) were added, and the mixture was stirred continuously for \(3\,\mathrm{h}\) [43]. Finally, \(80\,\mathrm{nm}\) of gold was thermally evaporated on top of the device as a back contact.
Atomic force microscopy (AFM) topography maps were acquired using a Veeco Dimension 3100 microscope in the tapping mode. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images were obtained using an FEI Helios
Atomic force microscopy (AFM) topography maps were acquired using a Veeco Dimension 3100 microscope in the tapping mode. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images were obtained using an FEI Helios NanoLab 600i microscope operating at \(5\,\mathrm{kV}\). X-ray diffraction (XRD) measurements were performed using a PANalytical Empyrean powder X-ray diffractometer under ambient conditions with \(\mathrm{CuK}\alpha\) radiation. The current density–voltage \((J{-}V)\) characteristics were measured using a solar simulator (Newport, USA) equipped with a 450-W xenon lamp (OSRAM) and a Keithley 2420 source meter. The \(J{-}V\) curves were measured under AM 1.5G illumination via a reverse (forward bias \((1.2\,\mathrm{V})\) \(\rightarrow\) short circuit \((0\,\mathrm{V})\)) scan. The step voltage and the delay time were fixed at \(20\,\mathrm{mV}\) and \(50\,\mathrm{ms}\), respectively. The active area of the solar cells was defined by a \(0.1\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) non-reflective metal mask. The photoluminescence (PL) spectrum was measured using Horiba Jobi-Yvon LabRAM ARAMIS at room temperature. A \(442\,\mathrm{nm}\) laser was used as the excitation source during the PL measurements. The time-resolved PL (TRPL) spectrum was measured using an Edinburgh FLS980 spectrometer with a \(485\,\mathrm{nm}\) laser as the excitation source.
## 3 Results and discussion
Figure 1(a) shows the chemical structure of PDVT-10 with diketopyrrolopyrole (DPP) units as electron-accepting blocks. The oxygen in the DPP unit interacted with the \(\beta\)-hydrogen atoms of the adjacent thiophene and formed hydrogen bonds readily. The as-formed hydrogen bonds strengthened the coplanarity of the main chain, resulting in strong intermolecular \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking. The incorporation of highly \(\pi\)-extended (E)-2(-2-(thiophen-2-yl)vinyl)thiophene (TVT) units extended the polymer coplanarity and promoted the intermolecular \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking. Additionally, the intermolecular interaction between the TVT, thiophene units, and DPP enhanced the overlapping of their molecular orbitals and thus reduced the distances between the polymer chains, which facilitated the charge-carrier transport. With long alkyl side chains (2-decyltetradecyl groups) on the bridging nitrogen atoms in the DPP unit to enhance the solubility, a flat PDVT-10 thin film could be easily obtained and exhibited a hole mobility as high as \(8.2\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}\cdot\mathrm{V}^{-1}\cdot\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) [41], which is higher than those of most of the reported HTMs for PSCs.
In addition to the high mobility, which satisfies
the demand for efficient charge extraction, the band alignment of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\), and PDVT-10 was energetically favorable for exciton dissociation and charge transfer at the interface, as shown in Fig. 1(b). The HOMO energy level of PDVT-10 was \(-5.28\,\mathrm{eV}\), yielding a sufficient driving force for hole transfer from the \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) absorber layer to the PDVT-10. The far lower HOMO energy level compared with regioregular poly(3-Hexylthiophene) \((-4.76\,\mathrm{eV})\) is supposed to improve the stability of the polymer against oxidation and thus enable excellent air stability [41]. Figure 1(c) shows a cross-sectional SEM image of a typical PSC. A \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) blocking layer \((30{-}40\;\mathrm{nm})\) was deposited on the FTO substrate via spray pyrolysis. A \(150{-}200{-}\mathrm{nm}\)-thick mesoporous \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layer (meso- \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)) was subsequently spin-coated on the substrate. A \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) absorber layer was prepared via a modified two-step spin-coating method [44]. As shown in the figure, the \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) perovskite formed an ordered and vertically aligned capping layer approximately \(400\,\mathrm{nm}\) thick on the meso- \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layer.
