The stabilities of three films under room light in the ambient conditions with \(20\%{-}40\%\) relative humidity were further investigated by optical inspection and XRD measurements. As shown in Fig. 3(a), the \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}/\) PTAA film began to degrade after \(5\,\mathrm{~d~}\) exposure and changed into yellow \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) after \(20~\mathrm{d}\) exposure. In contrast, the pristine \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) film was only slightly degraded after \(15\,\mathrm{d}\) . As expected, the \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}/\mathrm{PDVT-10}\) film remained unchanged after \(20~\mathrm{d}\) exposure under the same conditions. Figures 3(b)–3(d) show the corresponding exposure time-dependent XRD patterns, which experimentally indicate the degradation over time of the perovskite films with and without HTLs. The \(12.6^{\circ}\) peak is attributed to the (001) peak of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2},\) and the \(14.1^{\circ}\) peak is assigned to the (110) peak of \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) . We can use the relative strength of these two peaks to illustrate the degradation of \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) . As shown in Fig. 3(b), the intensity ratio of the \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) (001)
peak to the \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) (110) peak increased from \(\sim1{:}10\) to \(\sim1{:}3\) for the pristine \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) film after \(20~\mathrm{d}\) of exposure in the ambient conditions, indicating that a significant amount of \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) was decomposed into \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) . For the \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}/\mathrm{PDVT-10}\) film, the intensity ratio of the \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) (001) peak to the \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) (110) peak only increased from \(\sim1{:}10\) to \(\sim1{:}5,\) as shown in Fig. 3(c). For the \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}/\mathrm{PTAA}\) film, as shown in Fig. 3(d), such intensity ratio increased from \(\sim1{:}10\) to \(\sim2{:}1,\) which is consistent with the color change shown in Fig. 3(a). This experiment clearly indicated that the PTAA HTL accelerated the degradation of the perovskite layer, while the PDVT-10 acted as an effective protective layer for the \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) perovskite layer.
Figure 4(a) shows the PL spectra of the \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) films without or with different HTLs, including glass/ \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3},\) glass/ \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}/\mathrm{PTAA},\) and glass/MA/PDVT-10. While the PL emission positions are consistent for all
three films, the PL emission of MAPbI₃ is significantly quenched in both the glass/MAPbI₃/PTAA and glass/ MAPbI₃/PDVT-10 film. The similar significant PL quenching indicates that the efficient hole extraction at the MAPbI₃/PDVT-10 interface was as effective as that at the MAPbI₃/PTAA interface. TRPL measurements were performed to validate the charge transfer at the perovskite/HTLs interface. As shown in Fig. 4(b), the pristine MAPbI₃ exhibited a long PL lifetime of \(\sim62.9\,\mathrm{ns},\) while the PL lifetimes of MAPbI₃/PDVT-10 and MAPbI₃/PTAA were only \(\sim7.8\) and \(\sim8.8~\mathrm{ns},\) respectively. The large reduction of the PL lifetime suggests the effective hole extraction at the perovskite/HTL interface.
Considering the proper HOMO energy level, uniform film formation, and efficient hole-extraction properties of the pristine PDVT-10 film, we compared the PV performance of mesostructured PSCs fabricated without an HTL, with PDVT-10 as an HTL, and with PTAA as an HTL. Figure 4(c) shows the \(J{-}V\) curves for the champion devices under AM 1.5G illumination, and Table 1 summarizes the PV parameters of these devices. The best PDVT-10-based device exhibited a PCE of \(13.4\%\) , with an open-circuit voltage (Voc) of \(0.99\;\mathrm{V},\) a short-circuit current density (Jsc) of \(20.86\;\mathrm{\mA{\cdot}c m}^{-2},\) , and a fill factor (FF) of 0.65, while
the best HTL-free solar cell exhibited a far lower PCE of \(8.8\%\) , with a Voc of \(0.84\;\mathrm{V},\) a Jsc of \(19.47\;\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2},\) and an FF of 0.54. The best PTAA device exhibited the highest PCE of \(14.6\%\) , with a Voc of \(1.00\;\mathrm{V},\) a Jsc of \(20.20\;\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2},\) , and an FF of 0.72. Although the performance of the PDVT-10-based champion device was slightly lower than that of the best PTAA device, the overall performance of the PDVT-10-based devices was far better than the latter in terms of the average PCE and stability, as discussed below.
Figure 5 shows a histogram of the performance parameters for the three aforementioned types of devices (20 devices in each case), and Table 2 presents the PV parameters and the standard deviation. The HTL-free devices exhibited an average PCE of \(6.38\%\) , with a standard deviation of \(2.11\%\) , and the PTAA reference devices exhibited an average PCE of \(11.28\%\) , with a standard deviation of \(2.19\%\) . The PDVT-10-based devices exhibited an average PCE of \(12.97\%\) , with a small standard deviation of \(0.25\%\) , comparing favorably with the HTL-free and PTAA control devices. The major improvement of the PDVT-10 was due to the Voc and FF. The Voc was a result of the splitting of the electron and hole quasi-Fermi energy levels, which was triggered by the illumination and determined by the effective bandgap and carrier recombination. The
ideal Voc of the PDVT-10-based device is attributed to the matched HOMO energy level and the effective hole extraction across the perovskite/PDVT-10 interface with small serious resistance (Rs). The FF increase was associated with the reduced photogenerated charge recombination at the perovskite/PDVT-10 interface and the increased shunt resistance (Rsh). Because the Jsc is directly related to the spectral response and the collection probability, the slightly higher Jsc of the PDVT-10 based devices is attributed to the better hole collection of the PDVT-10 HTL. As shown in Table 2, all the PV parameters of the PDVT- 10-based devices exhibited a small deviation compared with the HTL-free and PTAA control devices, indicating the reliability and reproducibility of the PDVT-10 HTL devices. The larger variance of the PTAA devices may originate from the inhomogeneous dopant distribution.
## 4 Conclusions
We investigated the \(\pi\)-extended copolymer PDVT-10 as an ultrathin additive-free HTL for PSCs and achieved a PCE of \(13.4\%\). The PDVT-10 devices exhibited better device performance than PTAA-based PSCs and far better ambient stability. Unlike doped spiro-OMeTAD and PTAA, wherein the additive accelerated the degradation of \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\), the additive-free polymer protected the perovskite absorber layer, satisfying the prerequisite for the long-term stability of PSCs. Moreover, as a polymer family, PDVTs have excellent charge-transport characteristics, good solution processability, decent air stability, and thermal stability. The side chain and molecular weight of PDVTs can easily be modified to obtain the required properties; thus, PDVTs are promising for high-performance
additive-free HTLs for PSCs.
